        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="benefits">Benefit by benefit: which one wins?</h2>
      <p class="sub">Same goal, different tool. Here's who takes each round.</p>

      <div class="benefit">
        <h3>Muscle recovery &amp; soreness <span class="verdict v-cold">Cold plunge</span></h3>
        <p>Cold immersion reliably reduces <em>perceived</em> soreness (DOMS) in the 24&ndash;96 hours after hard training, and contrast (hot-cold) methods perform even better than cold alone. One caveat: a long cold plunge <em>immediately</em> after strength training may blunt muscle growth, so lifters chasing hypertrophy should wait ~1&ndash;4 hours before plunging.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit">
        <h3>Energy, mood &amp; focus <span class="verdict v-cold">Cold plunge</span></h3>
        <p>Cold water can spike norepinephrine by 200&ndash;500% and produce a sustained dopamine rise of roughly 200&ndash;250% that lasts for hours. That's the clean, drug-free "high" people get addicted to — and why a morning plunge is such a potent focus tool.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit">
        <h3>Relaxation &amp; stress wind-down <span class="verdict v-hot">Infrared sauna</span></h3>
        <p>Heat shifts you toward parasympathetic ("rest and digest") dominance. A sauna session is one of the easiest ways to down-regulate a wired nervous system at the end of a day.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit">
        <h3>Sleep quality <span class="verdict v-hot">Infrared sauna</span></h3>
        <p>An evening sauna elevates core temperature; the subsequent drop after you cool down mimics the body's natural sleep-onset signal. Cold, by contrast, is activating — plunging right before bed can backfire.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit">
        <h3>Heart health &amp; longevity <span class="verdict v-hot">Sauna</span></h3>
        <p>The strongest long-term evidence belongs to heat. Large Finnish cohort data links frequent sauna use to substantially lower cardiovascular and all-cause mortality (see the evidence section). Most of that data is on <em>traditional</em> saunas, so treat infrared as promising-but-less-proven here.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit">
        <h3>Metabolism &amp; fat <span class="verdict v-cold">Cold plunge</span></h3>
        <p>Regular cold exposure activates brown adipose tissue ("good fat" that burns energy to make heat) and can nudge up fat oxidation. Real, but a supporting act — not a weight-loss shortcut.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit">
        <h3>Circulation <span class="verdict v-both">Both — contrast wins</span></h3>
        <p>Heat opens vessels, cold clamps them. Alternate the two and you create a vascular "pump" that neither modality delivers alone — the core reason contrast therapy exists.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="benefit">
        <h3>Resilience &amp; discipline <span class="verdict v-cold">Cold plunge</span></h3>
        <p>Voluntarily walking into cold water and controlling your breath is a daily rep for your stress response. Few habits build "I can do hard things" quite as efficiently.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="pull">Cold sharpens you. Heat softens you. Contrast does both — and that's the whole idea behind ContrastMap.</p>

      <h2 id="choose">Which one should you choose?</h2>
      <p>If you can only pick one, match it to your primary goal:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Choose a cold plunge if</strong> you want a morning jolt of focus and mood, faster workout recovery, or a daily resilience practice.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose an infrared sauna if</strong> you want to relax, sleep better, ease muscle tension, or you simply find cold unbearable and won't stick with it.</li>
        <li><strong>Go with both (contrast) if</strong> you want the complete package — recovery, circulation, mood, <em>and</em> relaxation — and you're willing to build a short routine around it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Budget and space matter too: an infrared sauna blanket or a simple ice barrel each start well under a thousand dollars, while chiller-equipped plunge tubs and full sauna cabins run into five figures. If you're not sure you'll stick with it, <strong>try both at a studio before you buy</strong> — which is exactly what ContrastMap is for.</p>

      <h2 id="contrast">Why choose? The case for contrast therapy</h2>
      <p>Here's the thing the "vs" framing misses: the biggest benefits may come from using both, back to back. That's <strong>contrast therapy</strong> — deliberately alternating heat and cold.</p>
      <p>When you heat up, your blood vessels dilate. When you hit the cold, they constrict hard. Cycle between them and you train your vascular system like a muscle — a repeated expand-and-contract that pumps blood, may speed recovery, and leaves you feeling simultaneously calm and electric. Athletes have used contrast water therapy for decades; the sauna-and-plunge version is the same principle, dialed up.</p>
      <div class="call tip">
        <div class="h">&#128293;&#10052; The one rule everyone agrees on</div>
        <p><strong>Heat first, cold second.</strong> Warming up fully before the cold maximizes the vascular contrast, primes heat-shock proteins, and is gentler on your cardiovascular system than shocking a cold, constricted body with sudden heat.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="protocol">The contrast therapy protocol</h2>
      <p class="sub">A simple, evidence-informed template you can run today.</p>
      <div class="proto">
        <div class="pstep"><div><span class="t">Heat &middot; 10&ndash;20 min</span><br/><b>Sauna until you're fully warm and sweating.</b> Traditional saunas run 150&ndash;200&deg;F; infrared, 120&ndash;150&deg;F for a bit longer.</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><span class="t">Transition &middot; 30&ndash;60 sec</span><br/><b>Walk, don't run.</b> Towel off excess sweat, breathe, sip water.</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><span class="t">Cold &middot; 1&ndash;3 min</span><br/><b>Plunge to the shoulders at 45&ndash;55&deg;F.</b> Long, slow exhales help the cold-shock response pass.</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><span class="t">Rest &middot; 2&ndash;5 min</span><br/><b>Reheat or sit at room temp.</b> Let your heart rate settle. Don't shiver violently — that means you overdid the cold.</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><span class="t">Repeat &middot; 2&ndash;3 rounds</span><br/><b>Cycle back through.</b> Round two almost always feels easier than round one.</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><span class="t">Finish</span><br/><b>End on cold for daytime energy; end on heat before bed for sleep.</b></div></div>
      </div>

      <h3>Dial it to your level</h3>
      <div class="tbl-wrap">
        <table class="cmp">
          <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>&#10052; Cold</th><th>&#9832; Heat</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Beginner (wk 1&ndash;2)</td><td>30&ndash;60 sec &middot; 55&ndash;60&deg;F &middot; 2 rounds</td><td>10 min &middot; 150&ndash;160&deg;F</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Intermediate</td><td>1&ndash;3 min &middot; 50&ndash;55&deg;F &middot; 2&ndash;3 rounds</td><td>15 min &middot; 160&ndash;180&deg;F</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Advanced</td><td>2&ndash;4 min &middot; 40&ndash;50&deg;F &middot; 3&ndash;4 rounds</td><td>15&ndash;20 min &middot; 180&ndash;200&deg;F</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="call tip">
        <div class="h">&#127919; A useful weekly target</div>
        <p>A widely cited guideline (popularized from Dr. Susanna Søberg's work) is roughly <strong>11 minutes of cold and ~57 minutes of heat per week</strong>, split across 2&ndash;4 sessions — and to let your body rewarm itself after the final cold rather than jumping back into heat, if metabolic adaptation is your goal.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="evidence">The evidence: what research actually shows</h2>
      <p class="sub">Strong on the individual halves; still emerging on the exact combo.</p>
      <div class="call evidence">
        <div class="h">&#129656; Heat &amp; the heart</div>
        <p>In the long-running Finnish Kuopio (KIHD) cohort of 2,300+ men followed ~20 years, those using a sauna 4&ndash;7 times a week had markedly lower rates of sudden cardiac death and all-cause mortality than once-a-week users (Laukkanen et al., <em>JAMA Internal Medicine</em>, 2015). It's observational — not proof of cause — and was measured on traditional saunas, but it's among the most compelling wellness signals we have.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="call evidence">
        <div class="h">&#129504; Cold, mood &amp; metabolism</div>
        <p>Cold-water immersion produces large, well-documented increases in norepinephrine and a sustained dopamine rise (Šrámek et al., <em>Eur J Appl Physiol</em>, 2000). Cold exposure has also been linked to brown-fat activation and increased cold-induced thermogenesis (Søberg et al., <em>Cell Reports Medicine</em>, 2021).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="call evidence">
        <div class="h">&#128170; Contrast &amp; recovery</div>
        <p>Meta-analyses of contrast water therapy find it reduces delayed-onset muscle soreness and perceived fatigue after exercise, often as well as or better than cold alone. The vascular "pump" is the leading proposed mechanism.</p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>The honest caveat:</strong> most high-quality studies isolate <em>either</em> heat <em>or</em> cold. Rigorous trials on the specific sauna-then-plunge protocol are limited, and a lot of the popular claims run ahead of the data. The individual benefits are real; treat the combined ritual as a well-reasoned, enjoyable way to get both rather than a guaranteed formula.</p>

      <div class="call safety" id="safety">
        <div class="h">&#9888; Safety: who should be cautious</div>
        <p>Heat and cold are real cardiovascular stressors. Talk to your doctor first if you have heart disease, high or low blood pressure, are pregnant, or have a condition like epilepsy or Raynaud's. Never plunge alone, never combine with alcohol, ease in gradually, and get out if you feel faint or stop shivering-then-start slurring. This article is educational and not medical advice.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="home">Try it at a studio, or build it at home</h2>
      <p>You don't need to buy anything to start. The easiest on-ramp is a local studio with both a sauna and a plunge, where you can learn the rhythm safely and figure out whether you love it. When you're ready to make it a daily habit, a home setup pays off fast.</p>
