      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <details class="faq"><summary>Is a cold plunge or infrared sauna better?</summary><div class="a"><p>Neither is universally "better" — they do different jobs. Cold plunges win for alertness, mood, and recovery; infrared saunas win for relaxation, sleep, and circulation. For most people the best answer is to alternate both as contrast therapy.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>Should I do the sauna or the cold plunge first?</summary><div class="a"><p>Sauna first, cold second, in almost every case. Warming up fully makes the cold more tolerable, maximizes the vascular contrast, and is safer for your heart than entering heat while cold and constricted.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>Should you cold plunge before or after a workout?</summary><div class="a"><p>After is fine for general recovery. But if building muscle is your goal, avoid a long cold plunge in the ~1&ndash;4 hours right after strength training, since cold may blunt the growth signal. Heat is a safer immediate post-lift option.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>How often should you do contrast therapy?</summary><div class="a"><p>2&ndash;4 sessions per week suits most people. A common target is about 11 minutes of total cold and roughly an hour of heat per week, spread across those sessions. Daily is fine if you tolerate it and your recovery metrics stay healthy.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>Do I have to end on cold?</summary><div class="a"><p>End on cold for daytime energy, alertness, and metabolic benefit. End on heat if you're doing it within ~90 minutes of bed, because staying warm supports sleep onset. Match the finish to your goal.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>Is an infrared sauna as good as a traditional sauna?</summary><div class="a"><p>Infrared runs cooler and many find it more comfortable, but the strongest longevity data comes from hot traditional saunas. Both raise core temperature and deliver benefits; if cardiovascular research is your priority, traditional has the edge, while infrared wins on comfort and accessibility.</p></div></details>
      <details class="faq"><summary>How cold does the plunge need to be?</summary><div class="a"><p>45&ndash;55&deg;F (7&ndash;13&deg;C) is the productive zone for most people. Colder than ~40&deg;F adds cardiovascular risk without proportional benefit — the cold-shock response is similar at 50&deg;F, you just tolerate it longer. Beginners can start as warm as 60&deg;F.</p></div></details>

